      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Change-impact assessment and evidence retention distinguish justification of an assertion from continuing validity of evidence. Material influences include implementation, libraries, data, instruments, environments and governing assumptions. Delta changes material facts or dependencies, including invalidated assumptions; I(Delta) conservatively covers those facts and affected dependents; D*(t) covers transitive dependencies material to assertion, execution and reliance on check t.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 844,
          "quote": "Let Delta change material facts or dependencies, including discovery of an\ninvalid assumption. Let I(Delta) conservatively cover those facts and affected\ndependents, and D*(t) cover the transitive dependencies material to assertion,\nexecution and reliance on check t.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#STDO%20Foundations%2FConstruction%20assurance%2FChange%20impact%20and%20evidence%20retention",
          "start_line": 841
        }
      ],
      "key": "change_impact_retention",
      "label": "Change impact and evidence retention"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The workspace is continuing material construction state, including authoritative requirements, work products and durable records. Material external state is observed through its owning interface. Actor context is a bounded, fallible, disposable acquired account of those sources.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 904,
          "quote": "The workspace is the continuing material state of the construction, including\nits authoritative requirements, work products and durable records. Material\nexternal state is observed through its owning interface. The actor's context is\na bounded account acquired from these sources. It is fallible and disposable.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#STDO%20Foundations%2FWorkspace%20and%20context",
          "start_line": 901
        }
      ],
      "key": "workspace_context",
      "label": "Workspace and actor context"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Shared construction relation connecting purpose, action, observation and correction. A is bound current material state; its acquired account includes workspace state, authoritative external observations, justified knowledge and explicit unknowns, and may be incomplete or stale. Z is a selected set of required outcome and acceptance conditions, potentially satisfied by many states. Intermediate states S0 → S1 → … and a satisfying endpoint are distinct from Z. Domain strategy, actor configuration, execution mechanisms and additional assurance policy retain their owners.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 978,
          "quote": "A is the current material state bound for construction. Its acquired account\ndescribes material workspace state, relevant authoritative external observations,\njustified knowledge and explicit unknowns; that account can be incomplete or\nstale. Z is the selected set of required outcome and acceptance conditions;\nmany states may satisfy it.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#STDO%20Foundations%2FBounded%20construction",
          "start_line": 969
        }
      ],
      "key": "bounded_construction",
      "label": "Bounded construction"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A configuration allocates operations and information to capable actors, declares material dependencies between frames, and explains allocation in its existing design or frame record. Producing, checking and accepting are distinct responsibilities; functional decomposition, communication, authority and evaluation dependency are distinct relations.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1071,
          "quote": "A configuration allocates operations and information to capable actors and\ndeclares the material dependencies between their frames. It assigns effects\nonly within grants the allocator holds and may delegate. Its existing design\nor frame record explains the allocation against operations, dependencies,\ncapabilities, assurance, resource limits and failure assumptions.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#STDO%20Foundations%2FConfiguration",
          "start_line": 1067
        }
      ],
      "key": "actor_configuration",
      "label": "Configuration"
    },
    {
      "conditions": ["Arguments apply within the selected methodology domain and their stated instance conditions."],
      "definition": "Core contract derivation rows group decisions by their consequences. Linked bodies supply decisions; named consumers bind subjects and additional policy. Pillar contributions identify preserved relationships rather than deriving every domain policy from F1–F6.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1096,
          "quote": "The rows group decisions by the consequence they use. Their linked bodies\nsupply the decision; the named consumers bind its subject and any additional\npolicy. Each row states the instance conditions material to its argument.\nPillar contributions describe the relationship being preserved, not a claim\nthat every domain policy follows from F1–F6.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/codex/5592168e26759f37e730aed12c030a6061709ec4/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#STDO%20Foundations%2FCore%20contract%20derivations",
